{"id": "new-0", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 11.660204289635045, "ttft_seconds": 2.4005942049989244, "text": "# DNS Resolution and Negative Caching: Interaction, Worked Examples, and Limitations\n\n## 1. Foundations\n\nThe Domain Name System is a hierarchical, distributed database. A *resolver* (stub or recursive) walks that hierarchy to turn a name into data \u2014 typically an A/AAAA record, but also MX, TXT, NS, and so on. Every answer carries a **TTL** (time to live), telling caches how long they may reuse it. **Negative caching** is the mirror image: when a name *does not exist* or a record *does not exist for that name*, the resolver"}
{"id": "new-1", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 15.575580967705905, "ttft_seconds": 1.3656092210003408, "text": "# Copy-on-Write Memory in Operating Systems\n\nCopy-on-write (COW) is a memory management optimization in which multiple processes (or multiple mappings within one process) initially share the same physical pages, marked read-only. When any party attempts to write to a shared page, the hardware raises a protection fault; the kernel then transparently allocates a private copy of that page for the writer, updates its page tables, and resumes execution. The result is that memory is duplicated lazily\u2014only for pages that are actually modified\u2014rather than eagerly at the moment sharing is established.\n\nThis article explains the mechanism in detail, walks through"}
{"id": "new-2", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 16.52510762849367, "ttft_seconds": 1.1128933519939892, "text": "# How Database Indexes Affect Range Queries and Writes\n\nDatabase indexes are auxiliary data structures that let the engine locate rows without scanning an entire table. The most common index is a B-tree (or B+tree), which keeps keys in sorted order and supports both equality lookups and range scans in O(log n) time. This article explains how indexes change the cost of range queries and writes, with worked examples and a discussion of the limitations that make indexing a trade-off rather than a free lunch.\n\n## The B-tree as the underlying model\n\nA B-tree index stores key values in sorted order across pages. Each internal node holds"}
{"id": "new-3", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 15.949667000375644, "ttft_seconds": 1.3744576500030234, "text": "# OAuth Authorization Code Flow with PKCE: Mechanics, Worked Examples, and Limitations\n\n## Background: Why PKCE Exists\n\nThe OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow was designed for confidential clients\u2014server-side applications that can safely hold a client secret. Public clients (native mobile apps, single-page applications, desktop apps, CLI tools) cannot keep secrets: anything shipped to the user's device can be extracted. Historically, public clients either used the implicit flow (which exposes tokens in the URL fragment) or the authorization code flow without a secret, which left them vulnerable to a specific attack: **authorization code interception"}
{"id": "new-4", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 17.35214788890652, "ttft_seconds": 1.4118327789910836, "text": "# How Erasure Coding Reconstructs Missing Storage Shards\n\nErasure coding is a method of storing data across multiple storage locations (shards, chunks, or nodes) such that the data can be reconstructed even if some of those locations fail or become unavailable. It is widely used in distributed storage systems (HDFS, Ceph, S3, Azure Storage), object stores, and RAID-like schemes. The core idea is to add redundancy in the form of parity or coded shards, so that any *k* out of *n* total shards are sufficient to reconstruct the original data. This is often written as an"}
{"id": "new-5", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 14.921705172611984, "ttft_seconds": 1.0201627169881249, "text": "# Handling Clock Skew in Distributed Systems\n\n## Introduction\n\nA distributed system is a collection of independent computers that communicate over a network and appear to users as a single coherent system. One of the most subtle and persistent challenges in such systems is **clock skew**: the phenomenon where physical clocks on different machines disagree about the current time. Even with modern quartz oscillators and NTP synchronization, clocks drift at different rates due to manufacturing variance, temperature changes, and network delays in synchronization. Typical drift rates are on the order of 10\u2013100 parts per million (ppm), meaning a clock can gain or lose roughly 1\u201310 seconds per"}
